1. d4 { The Blumenfeld Gambit is also a very good opening for aggressive players. I'm not really an aggressive player, so I use the Queen's Indian instead, but I thought I'd include the opening. } 1... Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 { With this move, white isn't allowing a direct pin. } { [%csl Rb4][%cal Rf8b4] } 3... c5 { Very exciting move. } { [%csl Rc5] } 4. d5 { [%cal Gd4d5] } 4... b5 { This is known as the Blumenfeld Gambit, which is similar to the Benko Gambit and has a similar layout to the Nimzo-Indian. } { [%csl Rb5][%cal Rc4b5] } 5. dxe6 fxe6 6. cxb5 d5 { Wonderful center similar to the Colorado Gambit. } { [%csl Ge6,Gd5,Gc5] } 7. e3 { From here the piece layout is similar to the Nimzo-Indian layout. } 7... Bd6 { [%csl Bd6][%cal Bf8d6] } 8. Be2 O-O { [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 9. O-O Bb7 { [%csl Bb7][%cal Bc8b7] } 10. b3 Qe7 { [%csl Be7][%cal Bd8e7] } 11. Bb2 Nbd7 { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bb8d7] } 12. Nc3 Rad8 { [%csl Bd8][%cal Ba8d8] } 13. Qc2 e5 { Black has a very wonderful position in compensation for the pawn. Two bishops strongly looking on the kingside, the open f-file and the wonderful center. } { [%csl Bc5,Bd5,Be5,Rb5][%cal Gb7h1,Gd6h2,Rf8f2] } *


